**On 1 — yes, exactly.** And there's a detail that strengthens your point: import only looks back 30 days anyway (`LookbackDays`), so the transcripts it actually imports are all recent — the population most likely to have `gitOperation` records. So per-turn real-commit linking is worth building, and the rule is the one you just restated: record exists → sha resolves → reachable from default branch → use it; otherwise the head anchor. That's a CLI-side change (smarter value into the same `commit_sha` field), no schema or server difference.

**On the constraint — concrete version.** There are two ways the server engineer could write the ingestion code, and both make imports work today:

```
A)  if (checkpoint.kind === "imported") { do the whole linking pipeline }
B)  if (checkpoint.metadata.commit_sha) { link checkpoint to that commit }
    if (checkpoint.kind === "imported") { label it imported, attribute to importer }
```

The difference only appears later. If we ever put `commit_sha` on anything that *isn't* an import — say normal checkpoints start carrying a reverse link, or some new source of checkpoints appears — version B ingests them with zero server changes, because the linking was keyed on "does the metadata name a commit." Version A needs the server touched again, because the linking was keyed on "is this an import."

That's all it is: one sentence of guidance to whoever writes the server code — *link on the field, use `kind` only for labeling/attribution* — so the linking mechanism stays general instead of getting welded to imports. It costs nothing now; it just avoids a rework later. If it still feels abstract, it's genuinely a small thing — B is barely different code — it's just cheap insurance worth writing down.

